WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.450
The Hulk is capable of much more than many
fans realize, and not just in terms of power,

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.740
but in depth of character.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:11.860
The Green Goliath has a pretty complex history,
one with certain tragic aspects that are directly

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.700
responsible for his tendency to want to, well...

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.800
Until he found cinematic success as a member
of the Avengers, the Hulk's biggest pop culture

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.960
touchstone was the live-action Incredible
Hulk TV show from the '70s.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.260
On the small screen, the man-monster was nowhere
near as powerful as the comic book or movie

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.000
versions, so there's one aspect of the Hulk
that might come as a surprise.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.500
He's always been hard to beat, but it turns
out that even if he loses a fight, he'll be

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:42.000
back.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.180
It's not that he won't die, it's that he can't.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:49.460
The Hulk possesses a healing factor that makes
Deadpool's look like a band-aid.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.320
He's survived every kind of death you can
imagine, from getting used as a pincushion

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.300
by Wolverine to even having Bruce Banner's
body dissected and put into jars.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:03.420
In 2002's Incredible Hulk: The End, it takes
Hulk less than 20 minutes for his body to

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:08.670
regenerate after being devoured by mutated
cockroaches, which is pretty handy when you're

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.720
living in a world with, you know, mutated
cockroaches.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:17.200
In 2005's Incredible Hulk #77 we learn that
his body has evolved to the point where he

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.520
can safely extract oxygen from water, allowing
him to stay underwater as long as he wants,

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.160
and he can even survive in the vacuum of space
for a pretty long time.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:32.580
It was in 2018's Avengers #684 that we learn
that even death can't stop the Hulk.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.760
Plenty of heroes have come back from the dead,
but most of them wait a while.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.500
The new Immortal Hulk series, on the other
hand, has shown us that even if you can kill

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:45.360
him, he'll just come back as soon as night
falls, and he'll be angry.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:51.900
While his alter-ego might be a brilliant scientist,
the Hulk isn't exactly known for his intelligence.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:54.040
That doesn't mean that he's always stupid,
though.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.020
For one thing, there are so many versions
of the character, and some of them are literal

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.100
rocket scientists.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.080
In his origin story, the very first version
of the Hulk isn't going to win Jeopardy anytime

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.260
soon, but he can speak in full sentences that
aren't just about smashing.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:13.460
The gray Hulk is clever and crafty, outthinking
his foes as often as he overpowers them.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:18.180
Then there's the merged "Professor" Hulk,
who shares Banner's genius, the "Doc Green"

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:23.960
Hulk that we saw in 2014, and even the evil
mastermind called the Maestro, who originates

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.320
in a dystopian future.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.360
On the other hand, even the supposedly less
intelligent versions of the character sometimes

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.320
only seem stupid because we can't see through
their eyes.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:39.080
For example, in 2012's Indestructible Hulk
series, writer Mark Waid hardly bothered giving

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.580
the Hulk any dialogue at all, but not because
of a lack of smarts.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.900
This Hulk is more like a force of nature,
a creature of action completely beyond words.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.840
If you're a giant green titan who can take
on all comers in an outer-space gladiatorial

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.780
arena, what do you have to say to anyone?

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.140
Besides, you know, "somebody please get me
a new pair of pants."

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.480
"That's naked… that's very naked."

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.980
It's easy to go back to those early stories
and think that the Hulk was a brand new kind

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.180
of character whose stories captivated an audience
that had never seen anything like him.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.571
The truth, however, is that Bruce Banner was
not the first Hulk in the pages of Marvel

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:18.320
comics.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.120
In fact, he wasn't even the second… or the
third.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:26.700
Hulk version one was a giant robot in 1960's
Strange Tales #75.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.840
Next came Xemnu, the "Living Hulk," a giant,
furry alien who could control the minds of

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.440
multiple humans at once.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:38.220
Later, after the more Incredible Hulk became
a Marvel superhero, Xemnu went through a name

00:03:38.231 --> 00:03:43.090
change and started being billed as the "Living
Titan" in his very rare appearances, which

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:46.040
usually involved getting beaten up by the
Incredible Hulk.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.370
The final Hulk to precede the one we all know
and love was a sea monster in 1961's Tales

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.240
to Astonish #21.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.100
Kind of.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:58.180
See, the Hulk of "The Silent Screen" is a
character in a movie.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.260
The story fools you into thinking you're looking
at an audience watching a movie called The

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:02.760
Hulk.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:06.860
When the sea monster steps out of the movie
screen, you learn a different audience is

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:11.460
watching a movie about another audience being
scared by a sea monster who emerges from the

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.560
film, but it doesn't end there.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:18.620
You eventually learn you, the audience of
the comic, are watching an audience that is

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:23.060
watching another audience who is watching
still another audience, with the final audience

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.560
being the ones freaking out about the sea
monster.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.660
That's astonishing, all right, but maybe not
in the way they thought.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:35.520
When the Hulk first smashed his way into the
panels of Marvel Comics in 1962, his skin

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.300
was gray instead of the now-familiar green,
something that changed in the second issue.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:41.200
The reason?

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:46.580
Stan Lee chose gray over the suggestions of
colorist Stan Goldberg, who argued gray was

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.900
too difficult to keep a consistent shade.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:54.720
Lee wasn't convinced until Hulk #1 was released
and he saw what Goldberg was talking about.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:59.420
In the mid '80s, once coloring technology
had gotten a whole lot better, the gray Hulk

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:00.660
got a new lease on life.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:07.300
Writer/artist Al Milgrom resurrected this
version in 1986's Incredible Hulk #324.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.940
When Peter David took over writing the title,
he ran with it, and the gray, crafty Hulk

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:13.380
revitalized the series.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:18.040
In that classic storyline, the Hulk began
working as an enforcer for a casino owner

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:23.420
and mobster in Las Vegas, giving this version
his own unique name: Joe Fixit.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:28.160
In 2008, the Hulk title was rebooted with
a malevolent Red Hulk, who was eventually

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:32.500
revealed to be Banner's longtime enemy, Army
General "Thunderbolt" Ross.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.920
Another military man, Robert Maverick, later
assumes the title and powers of the Red Hulk,

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.800
sporting an incredible gamma-powered mustache
and mirror shades, until the Immortal Hulk

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.150
drains the gamma radiation out of him in Avengers
#685.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:47.280
The one and only?

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.520
The Red Hulks aren't the only different versions
of the gamma-powered goliath running around

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:53.680
the Marvel Universe.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:57.420
While Bruce Banner's dark side will always
be at the center of the story, there's been

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.500
a whole roster of Hulks running around at
one time or another.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:05.840
The first was Banner's cousin Jen Walters,
better known as She-Hulk, who got her powers

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:07.240
from a blood transfusion.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:11.860
Banner's wife Betty was the Red She-Hulk for
a time, and there's even a third She-Hulk

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:16.620
named Lyra who comes from a dystopian reality
where men and women are in a constant state

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:17.220
of war.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.200
Technically speaking, she's Bruce Banner's
daughter.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.680
There's also Brawn; a.k.a. Amadeus Cho, who
used to be known as the "Totally Awesome"

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:26.180
Hulk.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:30.940
And then there's a son of Hulk, and another
son of Hulk, and the Hulk's original sidekick

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:33.700
Rick Jones was once the blue-scaled A-Bomb.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:39.310
He was eventually "cured" by Doc Green before
being executed in the pages of Secret Empire,

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:42.720
but compared to the rest of Rick's life, that
wasn't all that unusual.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:47.400
He was once the new Bucky for a hot minute
and used to be half of Captain Marvel, too.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.260
We all know the rule about the Hulk: don't
get Bruce Banner angry, and you won't have

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.220
to worry about the "Other Guy," right?

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.020
"That's my secret, Cap…

00:06:58.660 --> 00:06:59.500
I'm always angry."

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:04.120
Not quite.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:08.360
Back in the early days, he worked more like
a werewolf, emerging from Banner at night

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:09.800
and only at night.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.360
In the late '80s, writer Peter David stressed
that Hulk was more than just a manifestation

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:18.060
of Banner's rage, he represented Banner's
survival instinct.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:24.650
In David's finale on the title, 1988's Incredible
Hulk #467, Banner attempts to end his own

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:29.670
life several times in the wake of his wife's
death, including a swan dive off the Empire

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:33.320
State Building, and the Hulk shows up to save
him every time.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:37.200
You don't even have to read comics to be familiar
with this particular idea.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.440
In the Avengers movie, Bruce Banner confesses
to the team that he attempted to do the same

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:43.580
thing, but was stopped by his other self.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:50.260
"So you're saying that the Hulk, the Other
Guy, saved my life?

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:51.440
That's nice."

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:56.580
Al Ewing, writer of Immortal Hulk, brought
back the day/night dynamic more recently and

00:07:56.580 --> 00:07:59.580
added a brand new trigger for the transformation:
death.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.860
Bruce Banner can only die during the day,
but rises again as the Hulk once the sun goes

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:05.360
down.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:07.120
The night is his time.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:11.800
When it comes to the question of how strong
the Hulk is, there's no definitive answer.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:17.080
Even after he transforms, the Hulk's strength
continues to increase, and the angrier he

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.680
gets, the stronger he gets.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:23.320
As long as he's on his feet and he's fighting,
there's virtually no limit to his power.

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:25.240
"Welcome, Strongest Avenger."

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:25.880
"Uh, what?"

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:29.980
This is why the wilder versions of the Hulk
tend to do more damage.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:34.060
When the green guy has a degree of Banner's
intelligence, he isn't always willing to let

00:08:34.079 --> 00:08:38.229
himself get angry, but when the Hulk doesn't
have Banner holding him back, he becomes a

00:08:38.229 --> 00:08:40.180
massively destructive force.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:44.940
Ironically, though, one of his more memorable
feats of strength came when Banner was in

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:45.460
control.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:50.220
In the pages of Secret Wars, he holds up a
mountain range on his back, saving most of

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:52.120
Marvel's heroes from being crushed.

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:53.160
The trick?

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:57.440
It happens because of Reed Richards, who keeps
insulting Hulk in order to keep him strong

00:08:57.460 --> 00:08:59.620
enough while the heroes find a way to escape.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.920
He's a founding member of the Avengers on
both the comics page and the silver screen,

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:09.230
a part of the original lineup of the Defenders,
and he even spent some time in a strange lineup

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:11.160
called the New Fantastic Four.

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:15.320
With all that in his history, it seems like
the Hulk is a good teammate to have around

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:16.700
when the bad guys come calling.

00:09:17.069 --> 00:09:21.320
Well, while you definitely do not want to
be on the side fighting against him, the truth

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:24.920
is that the Hulk just does not play well with
other superheroes.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.129
"You were one of the Avengers, one of the
team, one of our friends.

00:09:28.129 --> 00:09:30.160
This is what friends do, they support each
other."

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.380
"You're Banner's friend."

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.900
"I'm not Banner's friend, I prefer you!"

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:36.560
He'll help them, but the alliance is always
an uneasy one.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:40.790
In spite of helping to found the Avengers,
for example, he quit the team in the second

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:43.820
issue, and has fought the team more often
than he's been on it.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.850
The Hulk's contempt for other superheroes
is obvious even when one of his more intelligent

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:50.120
personas is in charge.

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:54.960
For example, the "Professor" Hulk had the
reins during the original Infinity Gauntlet

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:55.460
storyline.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:00.300
This version of the Jade Giant is known for
his intelligence and relative calm, but he

00:10:00.319 --> 00:10:04.660
still spends his entire time with the assembled
heroes insulting them, and even does some

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:07.140
ill-advised trash-talking against Thanos.

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:12.620
Whether he's green or gray, whether he talks
like a caveman or like a Nobel prize candidate,

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:17.679
you can be sure of one thing about the Hulk:
whoever you are, he probably doesn't like

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:18.180
you.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.720
There are plenty of different interpretations
of the Hulk, but one of the biggest changes

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:25.760
to happen over the years is the way his origin
story has developed.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:30.819
The Gamma Bomb is always a big part of it,
the catalyst for releasing the monster inside

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:31.420
Bruce Banner.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:37.320
But ever since we learned about Bruce's tortured
childhood in Incredible Hulk #312, there has

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:40.059
been a completely different layer to their
relationship.

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:44.540
The Hulk was with Bruce Banner long before
he was exposed to the radiation that gave

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:45.180
him a form.

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:49.740
The gamma bomb just opened the door for something
that had been inside Banner since he was a

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:50.240
boy.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:57.179
In 1991's Incredible Hulk #377, Bruce Banner
is officially diagnosed with Multiple Personality

00:10:57.180 --> 00:10:58.640
Disorder for the first time.

00:10:59.089 --> 00:11:03.999
According to Doc Samson, the Marvel Universe's
resident super-psychiatrist, both the gray

00:11:03.999 --> 00:11:08.459
and green Hulks are splintered personalities
that Banner's subconscious created to protect

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:10.640
him from the trauma he suffered growing up.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:15.819
Ever since that comic, more personalities
have emerged, including a huge, malevolent

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:16.960
version called the Devil Hulk.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:22.020
It's this last one who appears to be ruling
the roost in the horror-themed pages of Immortal

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:22.520
Hulk.

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:27.559
During Planet Hulk, the story a big chunk
of Thor: Ragnarok was based on, writer Greg

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:30.700
Pak presented an interesting take on Hulk's
view of Banner.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:35.589
As the Hulk's new gladiator allies tell their
life stories to each other, the Hulk shares

00:11:35.589 --> 00:11:36.080
his own.

00:11:36.629 --> 00:11:40.300
When he tells them of the gamma bomb that
set him loose from Banner, he not only says

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:44.220
the bomb didn't create him, he claims Banner
made the bomb to kill him.

